    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1881 as a Texas and Pacific Railway station, Pecos grew into a cow town where old cattle and wagon trails met.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Pecos-Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Perched on the west bank of the Pecos River, the town lies at the eastern edge of the Chihuahuan Desert, just south of New Mexico.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecos%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pecos is famed for its cantaloupe orchards, and the annual Cantaloupe Festival celebrates the region’s agricultural bounty.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Pecos-Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "As the largest city in Reeves County, Pecos serves as the county seat and regional commercial hub for ranching, oil, and agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecos%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Reeves County Detention Complex, operated by GEO Group, sits in Pecos and is the world’s largest private prison.",
